{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 4905690,
  "courseSlug": "javascript-data-structures-and-algorithms",
  "courseTitle": "100 Days of Code: JavaScript Data Structures and Algorithms",
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "45805713",
      "title": "Graph Terminology",
      "lectureOrder": 240,
      "videoOrder": 238,
      "sourceHash": "59a0f9de8f5238ee22dde6e54b0edfcc28a156655fbeffde9a66076f4691399e",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 5.15,
          "end": 10.55,
          "en": "Now, before continuing to the details of the graph data structure, we need to learn about the graph",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 10.55,
          "end": 15.29,
          "en": "terminology, because when you are asked questions in the interviews, they are going to ask the question",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 15.29,
          "end": 17.51,
          "en": "based on the graph terminology.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 17.51,
          "end": 21.26,
          "en": "So that's why it's better to understand the terminology of the graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 21.26,
          "end": 23.18,
          "en": "Now we have a typical graph here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 23.21,
          "end": 24.92,
          "en": "Now the first terminology is vertices.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 24.92,
          "end": 29.75,
          "en": "As we mentioned before, these are the nodes or vertices that we have in circles over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 29.75,
          "end": 32.63,
          "en": "So we have five nodes over here a, b, c, d, e.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 32.66,
          "end": 35.63,
          "en": "Now the lines between these nodes are called edges.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 35.63,
          "end": 38.99,
          "en": "So with edge these nodes are connected to each other.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 38.99,
          "end": 41.45,
          "en": "Now the next terminology is unweighted graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 41.45,
          "end": 48.02,
          "en": "Now if the graph does not have any associated weight with any edge in this case we can call this unweighted",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 48.02,
          "end": 52.67,
          "en": "graph, because for this edge, you see that we don't have any weight associated with it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 52.67,
          "end": 57.53,
          "en": "So from A to D we don't know how many units it takes to come from A to D.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 57.56,
          "end": 60.26,
          "en": "Now in some graphs we will have over here weight.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 60.26,
          "end": 66.77,
          "en": "So if we have any weight in the any edge of the graph it means that this is weighted graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 66.77,
          "end": 71.81,
          "en": "Even if we have one weight associated with any edge this is going to be called weighted graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 71.84,
          "end": 74.36,
          "en": "Now the next terminology is undirected graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 74.36,
          "end": 79.85,
          "en": "In this case, if you don't have any direction between the nodes in their edges, it means that this",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 79.85,
          "end": 81.05,
          "en": "is undirected graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 81.05,
          "end": 84.65,
          "en": "So from a we can go to d and from d we can go to a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 84.68,
          "end": 86.12,
          "en": "So we don't have any direction.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 86.12,
          "end": 87.91,
          "en": "So that's why it's undirected graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 87.94,
          "end": 91.48,
          "en": "Now, if you have a direction in this case it's going to be a directed graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 91.48,
          "end": 97.45,
          "en": "So we can easily come from A to D, but from D we cannot go to A because there is only one way over",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 97.45,
          "end": 97.69,
          "en": "here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 97.69,
          "end": 101.53,
          "en": "So this arrow over here shows that we can only come from A to D.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 101.56,
          "end": 104.56,
          "en": "We cannot go to back directly from D to A.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 104.59,
          "end": 110.77000000000001,
          "en": "Of course we can use the alternative path to go to E, B and A, but from D to A there is no direct",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 110.77000000000001,
          "end": 113.17,
          "en": "path because this is a one way edge.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 113.17,
          "end": 114.64,
          "en": "So this is a directed graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 114.64,
          "end": 117.37,
          "en": "Now the next terminology is cyclic graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 117.37,
          "end": 121.57,
          "en": "So if the graph has at least one loop in this case this is cyclic graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 121.57,
          "end": 126.4,
          "en": "So in this case the first loop is from a we can go to c d then come back to a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 126.43,
          "end": 127.75,
          "en": "We see that we have a cycle.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 127.75,
          "end": 129.52,
          "en": "And here we have another cycle.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 129.52,
          "end": 133.18,
          "en": "From A we can go to D, then E, then B, then again a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 133.21,
          "end": 134.83,
          "en": "So we have two cycles over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 134.83,
          "end": 136.12,
          "en": "So this is a cyclic graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 136.12,
          "end": 139.99,
          "en": "Now this one is a cyclic graph because we don't have a cycle over here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 139.99,
          "end": 144.82,
          "en": "So as you can see a graph with no loop is called acyclic graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 144.85,
          "end": 146.59,
          "en": "Now the next terminology is the tree.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 146.59,
          "end": 149.65,
          "en": "So we have discussed about trees in our previous lectures.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 149.65,
          "end": 153.46,
          "en": "So tree is a special case of directed acyclic graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 153.46,
          "end": 157.21,
          "en": "So as you can see from here we have a direction from A to C.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 157.24,
          "end": 159.97,
          "en": "We can go only from A to C and a to B.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 160,
          "end": 163.32999999999998,
          "en": "We cannot come back from B to A or C to a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 163.36,
          "end": 165.79,
          "en": "So this is a special case of graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 165.79,
          "end": 168.55,
          "en": "So tree is a special case of graph.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 168.55,
          "end": 174.07,
          "en": "So these are the main terminologies that you need to remember when you are dealing with graphs.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
